00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.790
through the structure and
components of the BigQuery

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.380
resource model.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.680
In this episode, we're
drilling in deeper on tables,

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.780
covering each of the
different table types

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.340
and when to use them.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.477
Stay tuned.

00:00:15.477 --> 00:00:18.339
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:23.670
So what is a table?

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.160
A BigQuery table
is a resource that

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.110
lives inside of a data set.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.890
It contains individual
records organized into rows,

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.160
with each record composed
of columns or fields

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.180
that have an enforced data type.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.830
BigQuery supports many
different data types,

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:43.730
including decimal
representations for precision

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:48.980
in financial use cases,
geography for geospatial data,

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.280
plus structs and arrays
for complex data.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:54.380
Oh, and keep an eye
out for more data

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.930
types that will support
semi-structured data, coming

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:59.120
soon.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.460
Aside from simply
storing your data,

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.980
tables can also be
used to control access

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:07.610
to information and store
metadata, such as descriptions

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:09.780
and labels.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.590
There are several different
types of tables in BigQuery,

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:17.090
so let's go ahead and
dig deeper into each one.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:20.710
The first table type we'll
talk about is a Managed Table.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:23.260
Managed Tables are tables
where data is saved

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:25.810
in BigQuery's native storage.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:30.100
Let's jump into the console
and create a new Managed Table.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.870
When getting started
with BigQuery,

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.880
you might already have
some data that you

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.540
want to load into a table,
such as a CSV file living

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.370
on your computer.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:42.970
Here, you can specify a local
file and leverage schema

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:46.240
auto detect so that BigQuery
infers the name and data

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.660
type of each column.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.530
Alternatively, you may want to
create an empty table, where

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.340
new rows will be inserted
through data pipelines.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.160
In this case, you'll use
the "create table" statement

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.860
in a SQL query to
create a new Managed

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.340
Table with a specified schema
and a set of configuration

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.420
options.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.940
This is going to be
a pretty big table,

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.820
so let's take it
one step further

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.370
and include partition
and cluster keys

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.550
so that BigQuery stores the data
in a way that will make queries

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.490
much more performant.

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:22.740
Another great thing
about Managed Tables

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.860
is that you can use time travel
to access data that has been

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.360
changed in the past seven days.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.660
So no need to panic
if you accidentally

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.760
erase a row or a table.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:37.700
You can still query data that
was deleted or has expired.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.470
Now, what if your
data is already

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:42.380
stored somewhere else,
like a data lake located

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.750
in a Cloud Storage Bucket?

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.930
Other systems might also
be accessing this data,

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:52.040
say, Spark Workflows running
on a Dataproc cluster.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.620
If you took the approach
of replicating the data

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.430
in a Managed Table, you would
have two different versions,

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.680
one stored in Cloud
Storage and one

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:05.810
stored in BigQuery, which could
lead to mismatching analyses.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.880
This situation is
a good use case

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.610
for our second type of table.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:14.060
External Tables give you
the ability to query data

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.160
that's stored
outside a BigQuery,

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:19.540
preserving a single
source of truth.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.850
BigQuery currently
supports querying data

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:24.940
stored in Cloud
Bigtable, Google Drive,

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:27.910
and Cloud Storage, which
can include leveraging

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.490
Hive-based partitioning.

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:34.210
Let's create an External Table
that points to a JSON file

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:37.270
living in our Storage bucket.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.420
This time, you'll specify
that it's an External Table

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:45.210
and also specify the format
and file path for the data.

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:48.630
You can now join this data
onto other tables in BigQuery,

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:52.000
so long as it's stored
in the same region.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.210
Aside from creating an
External Table in BigQuery,

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.520
you can also create
a Connection,

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.730
which is more like
an external data set.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:04.480
A Connection links BigQuery
to a Cloud SQL database

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.110
and allows you to
create external queries.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:10.510
An external query
executes the query

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:13.240
in Cloud SQL and
returns results which

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:16.120
you can leverage in BigQuery.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.190
Both Connections
and External Tables

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:20.709
are examples of
Federation, meaning

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:24.310
BigQuery will go and fetch the
data from the external source

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.100
at the query's runtime.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.950
This offers a great
approach to ensure everyone

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:34.140
in your organization is working
off of the same data sources.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.810
However, you should keep in mind
that query performance probably

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.730
won't be as high
as querying data

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:42.970
in native BigQuery storage.

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:45.390
So far, we've talked
about tables stored inside

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:49.770
of BigQuery and tables
stored outside of BigQuery.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:52.980
But let's imagine that you want
to create an aggregate version

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.530
of an existing BigQuery table--

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.980
for example, a sum
of sales each day.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:01.320
You could create a
whole new Managed Table

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.170
using a SQL query,
but you would need

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.380
to rerun that statement each
time the aggregate table needs

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.470
to be refreshed.

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:12.860
A better option
is to use a View.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:17.400
Views are virtual tables that
are defined by a SQL query.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:21.210
In BigQuery, you can create
a Logical View, a View where

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:25.050
the defining query is
simply executed at runtime,

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.850
or you can create a
Materialized View,

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.740
which is re-computed
in the background

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:32.280
when the base table changes.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.490
Materialized Views are great
for improving performance,

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.820
because the results are
saved and reused, as opposed

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:43.010
to rerunning the query each
time you're leveraging the View.

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:45.110
And better yet,
BigQuery will actually

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:47.540
reroute queries
against your base table

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.910
to the Materialized
View in order

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:52.750
to make things more efficient.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.350
However, there are some
limitations on the SQL

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:56.710
you can use.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:01.170
But these are in flux, so keep
an eye out on future releases.

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.300
On the other hand,
Logical Views are

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:07.600
less restrictive in the terms
of SQL that you can use.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.840
Logical Views are also really
useful for granting users

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:14.590
access to results of a query,
even if they don't have access

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:17.050
to the underlying data set.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:19.720
This is known as
an Authorized View.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:24.040
For more details, check out our
previous video, linked below.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:24.880
Congrats!

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:26.950
Now you know all the
different table types

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:30.460
in BigQuery and some best
practices on when to use them.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:33.160
For more guidance on getting
started with BigQuery

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:36.250
or digging deeper into
BigQuery resource concepts,

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:37.700
check out the links below.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:40.660
Thanks for joining us today
for "BigQuery Spotlight."

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:43.960
Watch out for the next episode,
and remember, stay curious.

